@@ PE1 | Predictive
On an aircraft programme, the engineering, manufacturing and certification teams each use the term "design freeze" differently, which is causing rework. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree a shared definition of the milestone and what it means for each team
B) Let each team keep its own definition, since each discipline has different needs
C) Ask the chief engineer to decide the definition and announce it by email
D) Remove the milestone from the schedule so that it no longer causes disputes
= A
? A common vision includes shared definitions of key milestones. Agreeing what "design freeze" means — and its implications for each team — removes a source of rework.

@@ PE1 | Agile
A new product owner joins midway through the release. The developers are unsure whether the priorities and direction will change. What should happen first?
A) The new product owner shares the vision and roadmap and discusses any changes
B) The team continues with the old backlog until the new product owner has fully settled in
C) The scrum master explains the vision to the new product owner, who then approves it
D) The developers decide the priorities themselves until the product owner is ready to lead
= A
? Continuity of vision is critical when key roles change. The product owner owns the vision and backlog and should align with the team early.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
A city festival relies on 400 volunteers who join only a week before the event. How can the project manager quickly build a shared sense of purpose and roles?
A) A briefing on purpose, key roles and how decisions are made on the day
B) A detailed manual that each volunteer must read before arriving at the festival site
C) Letting volunteers organise themselves on the day based on what they think is needed
D) Assigning volunteers to roles at random so that everyone has an equal opportunity
= A
? A concise briefing on purpose, roles and decision-making aligns people quickly; a manual alone or self-organisation on the day risks confusion.

@@ PE1 | Agile
Team members disagree about who the product's main users are, which leads to conflicting design decisions. Which technique would best build a shared understanding?
A) Creating user personas and journey maps together with the team, based on real user data
B) Asking each developer to design for the user group they consider most important
C) Letting the user interface designer decide alone who the main users should be
D) Postponing design decisions until the product has been released to the market
= A
? Personas and journey maps, grounded in user research, give the team a common picture of the customer and their needs.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
A bridge is being built through a public–private partnership. The public agency focuses on long-term safety, and the private contractor on profitability. What should the project manager do at the start?
A) Facilitate a partnering charter with shared objectives and joint success measures
B) Let each organisation pursue its own objectives within the contractual boundaries
C) Ask the public agency to define all objectives, since it represents the citizens
D) Focus only on the contract terms, since shared objectives cannot exist across firms
= A
? A partnering charter aligns organisations with different interests around shared objectives and success measures, complementing the contract.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
The vision was presented at the kickoff six months ago. Since then, new members have joined, and several cannot explain what the project aims to achieve. What should the project manager do?
A) Build the vision into onboarding and revisit it regularly at reviews and planning sessions
B) Send the original kickoff presentation to the new members and ask them to read it
C) Hold a second kickoff meeting only when the project enters its final phase
D) Assume that new members will learn the vision gradually from their colleagues
= A
? A vision must be reinforced continuously, especially as team membership changes, not communicated once.

@@ PE1 | Agile | multi
Which two practices best help a team agree on a shared definition of success? (Choose two.)
A) Defining a measurable product goal or objectives and key results
B) Agreeing release-level acceptance criteria with stakeholders
C) Setting individual velocity targets for each developer
D) Measuring success by the number of hours worked
E) Letting each member define success in their own terms
= A, B
? Shared, measurable goals and agreed acceptance criteria define success for everyone. Individual targets and hours worked encourage local optimisation and say little about value.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
The bank's compliance officer and the business lead disagree about whether a new product can launch with a simplified customer identification process. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a discussion that treats the regulations as fixed and explores options
B) Side with the business lead, since the launch date and revenue targets are critical to the business case
C) Side with the compliance officer and cancel the launch without exploring any alternative options
D) Ask the team to build both processes so that the decision can be made after the launch date
= A
? Regulatory requirements are not negotiable, but options within them can be explored collaboratively. Taking sides without analysis or duplicating work does not resolve the conflict.

@@ PE2 | Agile
Team members disagree strongly about how many days per week they should work from the office. Productivity and morale are suffering. What should the scrum master do?
A) Facilitate a team agreement on working arrangements within policy
B) Ask the functional manager to impose an office schedule for the whole team
C) Let each person decide independently, regardless of the impact on collaboration
D) Avoid the topic, since working arrangements are not related to delivery
= A
? Working agreements created by the team, within organisational policy, resolve the conflict and build commitment.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
The vendor's project manager and the internal project manager disagree about who must provide the test environments. Testing is now delayed. What should the internal project manager do first?
A) Review the contract and the responsibility matrix together with the vendor's project manager
B) Escalate to the sponsor and ask for the vendor to be penalised for the delay
C) Provide the environments internally and charge the cost to the vendor later
D) Wait for the vendor to provide the environments, since it is their problem
= A
? Agreements and responsibility matrices define who does what; reviewing them together is the objective first step.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
A disagreement between two department heads has turned into a contest in which each wants to win and is building alliances. Which approach is most appropriate at this level?
A) Negotiation, focusing on the parties' interests and acceptable trade-offs
B) Withdrawal, letting the contest run its natural course
C) Forcing a decision immediately without discussing the issue
D) Ignoring the alliances and continuing with the planned work
= A
? At higher conflict levels, where winning has become important, structured negotiation focused on interests is more effective than simple problem-solving or avoidance.

@@ PE2 | Agile
The product owner complains that the scrum master blocks access to the developers, while the scrum master says the product owner interrupts the team constantly. What should happen?
A) Clarify both roles together and agree how the product owner and developers will collaborate
B) Remove the scrum master's role, since the product owner should have full access to the team
C) Ask the sponsor to decide which of the two people is right about the team's interruptions
D) Ask the developers to choose whether they prefer to work with the product owner or the scrum master
= A
? The product owner and developers must collaborate closely; the scrum master protects focus without blocking collaboration. Clarifying roles and agreements resolves the tension.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Two team leads both want the most experienced engineer for their work packages, and the engineer cannot do both. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess both needs against project priorities and agree an allocation
B) Assign the engineer to the lead who asked first, to be fair to both of them
C) Let the engineer choose which work package to support during the next month
D) Split the engineer's time equally between the two work packages without analysis
= A
? Resource conflicts are resolved based on project priorities and impact, discussed openly with those involved.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
During a review, a team member presents a colleague's analysis as her own work. The colleague is upset. What should the project manager do?
A) Speak privately with her and ensure the colleague's work is recognised
B) Correct the record publicly during the meeting so that everyone knows who did the analysis
C) Ignore the situation, since the team as a whole is responsible for the analysis
D) Report the team member to human resources without first discussing the matter with her
= A
? Addressing the behaviour privately and restoring fair recognition maintains trust and respect without public humiliation.

@@ PE2 | Agile | match
Match each conflict resolution approach with the situation in which it is most appropriate.
1) Collaborate
2) Compromise
3) Force
4) Smooth
A) An important issue, with enough time to find a solution that satisfies everyone
B) Strong time pressure and parties with equal power
C) An emergency in which safety is at immediate risk
D) A minor issue where the relationship matters more than the outcome
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? The choice depends on the importance of the issue, time available, power and relationships. Collaboration is preferred when possible.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A developer works on stories alone, never asks for help and rarely shares progress, which often surprises the team. What should the scrum master do?
A) Coach the developer on collaboration and help the team agree on practices
B) Assign the developer only independent stories so that the lack of collaboration does not matter
C) Ask the developer to send the scrum master a written progress report at the end of each day
D) Report the behaviour to the developer's manager as a performance problem in the next review
= A
? Coaching individuals and reinforcing team practices — such as pairing and visible boards — builds transparency without micromanagement.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
Three weeks before an aircraft certification deadline, the test team shows signs of fatigue and is making more errors. What should the project manager do?
A) Review the workload, adjust shifts and discuss the schedule risk with the sponsor
B) Ask the team to keep going, since only three weeks remain before the certification deadline
C) Offer a large bonus to each tester if the certification deadline is met without any delay
D) Ask the quality department to double-check every test result produced by the tired team
= A
? Fatigue threatens safety and quality. A sustainable approach, combined with transparent discussion of schedule risk, is required.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
A senior manager insists on approving every task of an agile team, slowing work down. The manager believes people must be closely supervised. What should the project manager do?
A) Show the manager, with delivery data, how empowerment improves results
B) Ask the team to work around the manager by starting tasks before they are approved
C) Accept the approvals, since senior managers have the authority to supervise teams
D) Complain to human resources that the manager's style is unsuitable for agile teams
= A
? The manager reflects a Theory X view. The PM can influence by showing evidence that empowered, self-organising teams deliver better outcomes.

@@ PE3 | Agile
Sales staff constantly approach developers with urgent requests, disrupting sprint work. The team asks for help. What should the scrum master do?
A) Redirect requests to the product owner and agree an intake process with sales
B) Ask the developers to handle sales requests in the evenings after normal work
C) Ban sales staff from the team area for the rest of the release period
D) Let each developer decide individually whether to accept sales requests
= A
? Shielding the team and channelling requests through the product owner protects focus while keeping sales engaged.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
The sponsor offers a bonus if the team finishes a six-month project in three months. The team believes this is impossible and shows no extra motivation. Which theory explains this best?
A) Expectancy theory: the team doubts that effort can achieve the target
B) Herzberg's theory, because bonuses are always hygiene factors
C) McGregor's Theory X, because the team dislikes work and avoids it
D) Maslow's hierarchy, because the team's safety needs are unmet
= A
? According to Vroom's expectancy theory, motivation requires belief that effort leads to performance; an unattainable target removes motivation.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A senior developer offers to mentor two junior colleagues, but says the sprint workload leaves no time. What should the scrum master do?
A) Support the mentoring and account for it in sprint planning
B) Ask the senior developer to mentor the juniors after working hours
C) Postpone the mentoring until the current release has been completed
D) Ask the product owner to approve each mentoring session
= A
? Developing people is part of building a high-performing team; capacity for mentoring should be planned rather than squeezed out.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
Rumours of an organisational restructuring are affecting the team's morale and focus. What should the project manager do?
A) Share what is known transparently, acknowledge the uncertainty and keep the team informed
B) Tell the team that there is no restructuring, even though the project manager does not know
C) Avoid the topic completely so that the team focuses on its work
D) Ask the team to stop discussing rumours during working hours
= A
? Honest communication about what is and is not known builds trust and reduces anxiety more than silence or false reassurance.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
The project manager is overloaded with administrative tasks, while a capable team member wants more responsibility. What should the project manager do?
A) Delegate suitable responsibilities with clear authority and expected results
B) Continue handling everything personally to maintain full control
C) Delegate the tasks quickly without explaining the expected results
D) Ask the sponsor to hire an administrative assistant for the project
= A
? Delegation with clear authority and expectations develops people and frees the PM to focus on leadership and integration.

@@ PE3 | Agile | multi
Which two characteristics are typical of a high-performing team? (Choose two.)
A) Mutual trust and shared accountability for results
B) Open communication and commitment to shared goals
C) Reliance on individual heroics to meet deadlines
D) Avoidance of any disagreement to maintain harmony
E) A strict hierarchy for all technical decisions
= A, B
? High-performing teams combine trust, accountability, open communication and shared goals. Heroics, conflict avoidance and rigid hierarchy undermine performance.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A three-day music festival will be held in a city park. Residents nearby are worried about noise and traffic. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage residents early, listen to concerns and agree mitigation measures
B) Proceed as planned, since the city council has already approved the festival and its permits
C) Send residents a leaflet one day before the festival describing the expected disruption
D) Ask the police to handle any complaints from residents during the festival weekend
= A
? Early engagement with affected communities builds support and can lead to practical mitigation, reducing complaints and risks.

@@ PE4 | Agile
A bank wants regular input from customers on its new mobile app. Which engagement approach is most effective?
A) A customer advisory panel that reviews working increments every few weeks
B) An annual survey of all customers about the bank's digital services
C) Feedback collected only through the app store after each public release
D) Input from branch staff, who can represent the customers' views
= A
? Regular, direct engagement with real customers on working increments provides timely, high-quality feedback.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
A director publicly supports the project but appears to delay decisions and resources behind the scenes. What should the project manager do first?
A) Meet the director privately to understand their interests and concerns
B) Report the director's behaviour to the sponsor and request intervention
C) Confront the director in the next steering committee meeting
D) Exclude the director from decisions to avoid further delays
= A
? Hidden resistance often stems from unspoken concerns. A private conversation to understand interests is the first step.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A high-profile public infrastructure project attracts intense media interest, and team members are being approached by journalists. What should the project manager ensure?
A) A designated spokesperson and clear media guidelines in the communications plan
B) That every team member answers journalists' questions as openly as possible
C) That all media contact is refused to avoid any negative coverage
D) That the contractor's staff handle the media on the project's behalf
= A
? Designated spokespeople and guidelines ensure consistent, accurate public communication.

@@ PE4 | Agile
A senior stakeholder asks to attend the daily scrum every day to give the developers direction. What should the scrum master do?
A) Explain the purpose of the daily scrum and offer the sprint review and refinement for input
B) Agree, since senior stakeholders should be able to direct the developers whenever needed
C) Refuse without explanation, since only developers may attend the daily scrum
D) Cancel the daily scrum and replace it with a weekly meeting with the stakeholder
= A
? The daily scrum is for developers to plan their day. Stakeholders contribute through the product owner, the sprint review and refinement.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A regional politician has high power over permits and moderate interest in the project. How should the project manager engage this stakeholder?
A) Keep the politician satisfied with targeted updates and consult on matters affecting the region
B) Monitor the politician with minimal effort, since their interest in the project is only moderate
C) Manage the politician as a core team member and invite them to every project meeting
D) Avoid contact with the politician so that the project is not seen as politically influenced
= A
? A high-power stakeholder needs to be kept satisfied with relevant information and consultation, without overloading them.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
Three partner organisations deliver parts of a joint project, each with its own management. How should stakeholder engagement be organised?
A) Joint governance with an agreed engagement and communication approach
B) Separately by each partner, with the project manager receiving occasional summaries
C) Only through the contract managers of each organisation, at quarterly meetings
D) By the largest partner, which then informs the other two partners of decisions
= A
? Multi-organisation projects need joint governance and a shared approach to engagement to stay aligned.

@@ PE4 | Agile
In an agile project, the IT operations team that will support the product has not yet been involved. When should they be engaged?
A) Early and throughout, for example in refinement, so operational needs are built in
B) Only in the final sprint, when the product is ready to be handed over to them
C) After go-live, when they begin to receive support tickets from the users
D) Only if a serious problem occurs in production that the developers cannot fix
= A
? Early engagement of operations (as in DevOps practices) ensures supportability requirements are addressed throughout.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
An aircraft customer expects a weight target that the engineers say cannot be met with the current materials. What should the project manager do?
A) Share the engineering data with the customer and discuss trade-off options
B) Accept the target and ask the engineers to find a solution during testing
C) Change the weight target in the specification without informing the customer
D) Tell the customer the target will be met and address the issue later
= A
? Aligning expectations requires sharing evidence and agreeing options (for example, materials, cost or other trade-offs) rather than making unrealistic commitments.

@@ PE5 | Agile
The product owner and the sponsor disagree about the scope of the minimum viable product: the sponsor wants many features, the product owner wants a lean version. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a discussion based on the MVP's learning and business goals
B) Support the sponsor, since the sponsor is funding the development of the product
C) Support the product owner, since the product owner always decides on the product
D) Build both versions of the MVP and let the market decide which one is better
= A
? Anchoring the discussion in what the MVP must learn and achieve helps both stakeholders align.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
The client expects 5,000 attendees at a conference, but registration data and similar events suggest about 3,000. What should the project manager do?
A) Share the data and align the plan and budget on a realistic number
B) Plan for 5,000 attendees, since the client is paying for the conference
C) Plan for 3,000 attendees without telling the client about the change
D) Plan for 8,000 attendees to make sure there is enough capacity
= A
? Evidence-based alignment avoids overspending or under-provisioning and keeps the client informed.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
The customer believes the warranty period is 24 months, while the project team believes it is 12 months. What should the project manager do?
A) Check the contract's warranty terms and clarify them with the customer
B) Agree to 24 months to maintain a good relationship with the customer
C) Insist on 12 months without checking any of the documentation
D) Ask the legal department to inform the customer of the warranty period
= A
? The contract defines the warranty; checking it and clarifying with the customer resolves the misunderstanding objectively.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Users expect personalised recommendations in the first release, but the roadmap places them in the third release. What should the product owner do?
A) Explain the roadmap and the reasons for the order, and listen to the users' feedback
B) Move personalisation into the first release to meet the users' expectations immediately
C) Remove personalisation from the roadmap so that users stop asking for it
D) Avoid discussing the roadmap with users until the first release is complete
= A
? Transparency about priorities, combined with listening to feedback, aligns expectations and may inform reprioritisation.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
Executives expect a new banking chatbot to answer every customer question correctly. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree realistic accuracy targets and a handover to human agents
B) Promise full accuracy, since the AI vendor's marketing materials claim it is possible
C) Delay the launch until the chatbot answers every possible question correctly in tests
D) Launch the chatbot without discussing accuracy and let customers report any errors
= A
? AI systems have limitations. Agreeing measurable targets and a safe fallback aligns expectations and protects customers.

@@ PE5 | Predictive | multi
Which two actions best align stakeholders' expectations about quality? (Choose two.)
A) Agreeing measurable acceptance criteria
B) Reviewing samples or prototypes with stakeholders
C) Promising that there will be zero defects
D) Leaving quality undefined until testing
E) Letting the quality team decide alone
= A, B
? Concrete criteria and tangible samples create shared understanding of quality. Promises of perfection or undefined quality create disappointment.

@@ PE6 | Agile
A stakeholder is upset that a feature they requested was moved to a later release. What should the product owner do?
A) Explain the reasons for the change in priority and discuss options with the stakeholder
B) Move the feature back into the current release to restore the stakeholder's satisfaction
C) Ask the scrum master to explain the decision, since the scrum master manages stakeholders
D) Avoid the stakeholder until the current release is complete and the feature is delivered
= A
? Transparent explanation and dialogue maintain trust; priorities should not change simply to appease one stakeholder.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
The customer complains that the project raises too many change requests. What should the project manager do first?
A) Analyse the causes with the team and discuss them with the customer
B) Stop raising change requests and absorb the extra work within the current budget
